```javascript
// CommonJS (Node's original module system)
const { readFile } = require("fs");
module.exports = { greet: () => "hi" };

// ES Modules (the language standard)
import { readFile } from "fs";
export const greet = () => "hi";
```

| | CommonJS | ES Modules |
|---|---|---|
| **`require`/`import` runs** | synchronously, as a normal function call, anywhere in the code | as a hoisted, static declaration, resolved before execution |
| **Exports** | a mutable `module.exports` object, reassignable at runtime | a fixed, statically-analyzable list of bindings |
| **Where it originated** | Node.js (pre-ESM) | the ECMAScript language standard itself |
| **Browser support** | none natively — needs a bundler | native, via `<script type="module">` |

CommonJS treats a module load like calling a function that returns an object. ESM treats it like a declaration the engine can fully analyze without running anything.

Static `import` at the top of a file is resolved and bundled eagerly — every module it references ships in the initial bundle. `import()`, called as a function anywhere in the code, is the deliberate exception: it returns a **Promise** and lets the bundler split that module into a **separate chunk**, loaded only when the code path actually runs.

```javascript
button.addEventListener("click", async () => {
  const { openModal } = await import("./modal.js"); // fetched only on click
  openModal();
});
```

`modal.js` and everything it imports get bundled into their own file, fetched over the network only the first time this click handler fires — not as part of the initial page load. This is the mechanism behind route-based code-splitting in every modern framework (`React.lazy`, Vue's async components, Next.js's automatic route splitting) — they're all `import()` under the hood, wrapped in framework sugar.

```javascript
// utils.js
export function used() { return 1; }
export function unused() { return 2; }

// app.js
import { used } from "./utils.js";
console.log(used());
```

Because `app.js` only statically imports `used`, a bundler doing tree-shaking can prove `unused` is never referenced anywhere in the graph and **omit it entirely** from the final bundle — not just skip calling it, but not include its code at all.

![app.js importing only used() from utils.js while unused() is tree-shaken out](assets/tree-shaking-dependency-graph.svg)

```javascript
// Named exports — one file, many named things
export function debounce() {}
export function throttle() {}
import { debounce, throttle } from "./utils";

// Default export — one file, one primary thing
export default function Button() {}
import Button from "./Button"; // the importer can name it anything
```

| | Named exports | Default export |
|---|---|---|
| **Tree-shaking** | precise — a bundler knows exactly which named bindings are used | works, but slightly coarser — the whole default is one unit |
| **Import naming** | fixed at the source, consistent everywhere it's imported | importer picks any name, which can drift inconsistently across a codebase |
| **Refactoring** | renaming the export is a traceable, tool-assisted rename | renaming the source function doesn't force import sites to update, since names aren't tied |
| **Typical fit** | utility modules with several related exports | a component or class that is the one thing a file exists for |

Many style guides now prefer named exports even for single-purpose files specifically for the consistent-naming and tooling benefits — default exports remain common mainly by convention in component-per-file frameworks.

A "barrel" file — an `index.js` that re-exports everything from a directory (`export * from "./button"; export * from "./modal";`) — is convenient to import from, but can quietly defeat tree-shaking depending on the bundler and how the re-exports are written.

```javascript
// components/index.js — a barrel
export * from "./button";
export * from "./modal";
export * from "./huge-rarely-used-chart-library-wrapper";

// app.js
import { Button } from "./components"; // only wants Button
```

Some bundlers, especially with side-effectful modules or looser configuration, end up including the entire barrel's transitive dependency tree even though only `Button` was imported — because proving nothing else has a side effect requires more static analysis than a straightforward named import does. The practical guidance: import directly from the specific file (`from "./components/button"`) in performance-sensitive code, and check your bundler's `sideEffects` field configuration (`package.json`) if barrel files are unavoidable.
